  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThrough Friday: Below Average Temperatures and Mostly Dry Conditions\n\nMostly quiet weather continues through Friday as upper level high \npressure continues to meander around the southern High Plains into \nthe Desert Southwest. Northwest flow will remain over the forecast \narea during this time frame and temperatures will remain slightly \nbelow average in the 70s to lower 80s for highs. Mainly dry \nconditions are still expected for most of the area, however, a few \nrain showers or a storm may occur on Friday morning across \nnortheastern Iowa. Just enough H700 theta-e may reach northeastern \nIowa out ahead of a southeastward propagating shortwave to allow \nvery light rain showers to reach portions of the CWA. The 23/00z \nsuite of hi-res guidance is hinting at this potential, but given low \nconfidence in that scenario panning out, have kept PoPs below 10 \npercent. If any rain does occur, it would likely only amount to a \nfew hundredths at best.\n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Storm Chances Return\n\nMoisture throughout the column will continue to surge northward \ngoing into this weekend as the high pressure system to our southwest \nstrengthens and the ridge elongates well into Alberta and \nSaskatchewan by Saturday. Shortwave troughs are expected to \nround the ridge axis this upcoming weekend providing chances for\nthunderstorms, some of which could be severe. However, model \nguidance continues to show strong capping around H800 on \nSaturday through Monday which does leave high uncertainty in the\nsevere storm aspect for those days. Even with that being said, \nthunderstorm chances continue to trend up given the combination \nof increased moisture and PVA supplied by shortwaves moving over\nthe region. Timing and location of any potential storms is also\nanother area of uncertainty as model guidance does not agree \nwith the timing and placement of some features.\n\nSunday and Monday: Summer Heat Returns with Potential Heat Headlines\n\nThe increase in surface moisture along with the strengthening of the \nridge does come at a cost in terms of the hot conditions returning \nto the region. Low level WAA moves in this weekend but should be at \nits peak on Sunday and Monday with H850 temperatures in the lower to \nmid 20s Celsius. Model guidance yesterday was not too keen on heat \nindex values approaching 100 on Monday, however, that has quickly \nchanged over the last 24 hours. Now both Sunday and Monday are \nfeaturing at least 30-50 percent chances for heat index values to \nreach or exceed 100 based on the latest probabilities from long \nrange ensembles. Northeastern IA and southeastern MN currently have \nthe higher potential for heat related headlines on Sunday and that \nbecomes more widespread into southwestern and west central WI on \nMonday. Cloud cover and potential shower or thunderstorm activity \ncould lead to the air temperatures being a bit on the high side in \nthe NBM but will refrain from making any changes at this point due \nto high uncertainty.",
